@@ PE1 | Hybrid
A pharmaceutical company is digitising its laboratory records. The scientists see it as "an IT project" and show little interest in the planning sessions. What should the project manager do?
A) Co-create the vision with the scientists, focusing on the benefits for their work
B) Ask IT to finish the design first and present the completed system to the scientists
C) Ask the lab director to make attendance at planning sessions compulsory for all scientists
D) Proceed without the scientists, since the system will be configured by IT specialists
= A
? People commit to a vision they help shape and that addresses their needs. Mandating attendance or excluding the users leads to a system nobody wants to use.

@@ PE1 | Agile
The product owner sets each sprint goal alone before sprint planning, and the developers say they feel no ownership of it. What should the scrum master suggest?
A) Craft the sprint goal collaboratively during sprint planning
B) Ask the product owner to send the sprint goal by email a day earlier
C) Remove sprint goals, since the developers do not value them
D) Let the developers write the sprint goal without the product owner
= A
? In Scrum, the whole Scrum team collaborates to define the sprint goal during planning, building shared ownership while the product owner brings the value perspective.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
During a power plant maintenance shutdown, the contractors focus on finishing quickly, while the plant staff focus on avoiding any safety incident. What should the project manager do to create a shared direction?
A) Agree joint objectives with both groups, in which safety is treated as non-negotiable
B) Let the contractors set the pace, since the shutdown is costly for every day it continues
C) Let the plant staff decide everything, since they will operate the plant afterwards
D) Hold separate meetings with each group and avoid discussing their different priorities
= A
? A common vision integrates both priorities: completing efficiently within non-negotiable safety requirements. Siding with one group or avoiding the topic leaves the conflict in place.

@@ PE1 | Agile
A team wrote its charter nine months ago. Since then, half of the members have changed, and some of the original agreements are no longer followed. What should the team do?
A) Revisit and update the charter with the current members
B) Ask the new members to read and sign the existing charter
C) Let the project manager rewrite the charter for the new team
D) Discard the charter, since the original authors have left
= A
? Team charters are living agreements. When membership changes, the current team should review and update them so everyone owns the agreements.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
In a status meeting, it becomes clear that team members interpret the project's success criteria differently: some think on-time delivery matters most, others think user adoption does. What should the project manager do?
A) Clarify the agreed success criteria and their priority, using the charter
B) Let each team member continue to work toward the criterion they consider most important
C) Ask the sponsor to send an email listing the success criteria in a new order of priority
D) Postpone the discussion until the end of the project, when success will be measured
= A
? A shared understanding of the documented success criteria (and their relative priority) aligns decisions across the team.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
Four teams contribute to a digital ticketing programme, and each optimises its own goals. The sponsor wants all teams pulling in the same direction. Which approach would help most?
A) Define shared objectives and key results linked to the vision
B) Rank the teams each month according to their individual velocity
C) Let each team define its own vision and report it to the sponsor
D) Ask the PMO to set detailed task targets for every team member
= A
? Shared OKRs connect each team's work to common outcomes and the overall vision. Ranking teams by velocity encourages local optimisation.

@@ PE1 | Agile | match
Match each technique with its purpose in developing a common vision.
1) Elevator pitch
2) Product box
3) Team charter
4) Product roadmap
A) A concise statement of the product's value proposition
B) Designing imaginary packaging to focus on the key benefits
C) Agreeing the team's values and working agreements
D) Showing the high-level direction and themes over time
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? Each technique supports a shared vision in a different way: articulating value, focusing on benefits, agreeing how the team works and showing direction over time.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
The warehouse manager wants the new warehouse system to go live after the peak season, while the project schedule shows go-live during the peak. Both feel strongly. What should the project manager do?
A) Discuss the risks and the options with the warehouse manager in order to agree a go-live date
B) Keep the planned date, since the schedule baseline was approved by the steering committee
C) Move go-live after the peak season without analysing the impact on the rest of the project
D) Ask the sponsor to decide immediately without discussing the matter with the warehouse manager
= A
? Collaborative problem-solving with data about operational risks and project impacts can lead to an agreed date, possibly via change control. Ignoring the concern or moving dates without analysis is inappropriate.

@@ PE2 | Agile
The UX designer complains that developers implement screens without involving her, and the developers say her designs arrive too late. What should the scrum master do?
A) Facilitate agreement on how and when design work fits the workflow
B) Ask the product owner to decide whose approach is correct and enforce it from now on
C) Let the developers continue without the designer to avoid delaying the sprint work
D) Ask the designer to deliver all designs for the release before the next sprint starts
= A
? The conflict reflects a process problem. Facilitating an agreement — for example, including UX in the definition of ready or working a sprint ahead — addresses the root cause.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
The PMO demands detailed weekly reports from an agile team, which says the reporting takes time away from delivery. Tension between them is rising. What should the project manager do?
A) Negotiate tailored reporting with the PMO that uses the team's existing agile artifacts
B) Tell the team to comply fully with the PMO, since the PMO sets the organisation's standards
C) Tell the PMO that agile teams do not produce reports and should not be asked to do so
D) Produce the reports personally every weekend so that the team is not disturbed at all
= A
? A collaborative solution — such as dashboards generated from the team's tools — meets the PMO's oversight needs without burdening the team.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
Two senior engineers disagree about a minor drawing convention. The issue has no impact on the deliverable, and their working relationship is important for the next six months. Which approach is most appropriate?
A) Smooth the issue by emphasising areas of agreement
B) Force a decision using the project manager's authority
C) Escalate the disagreement to the engineering director
D) Ask the team to vote on the correct drawing convention
= A
? For trivial issues where relationships matter, smoothing (accommodating) can be appropriate. Forcing or escalating a minor matter wastes goodwill.

@@ PE2 | Agile
Testers want to add more criteria to the definition of done, while developers argue that a longer list will slow delivery. What should the scrum master do?
A) Facilitate a team agreement on a DoD that balances quality and flow
B) Accept the testers' criteria, since quality must always take priority over speed
C) Accept the developers' position, since they do most of the work on each story
D) Ask the product owner to write the definition of done for the team alone
= A
? The definition of done is the whole team's agreement. Facilitated discussion lets the team weigh quality risks and flow and reach shared commitment.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
Conflicts between two departments keep recurring over which deliverables the shared team should work on first. What is the most effective long-term action?
A) Have the sponsor clarify and communicate the priorities to both departments
B) Resolve each conflict individually as it arises during the weekly status meeting
C) Alternate priorities between the two departments every week to treat them equally
D) Ask the team members to choose their own priorities based on personal judgment
= A
? Recurring conflicts over priorities point to a root cause — unclear priorities. Clarifying them at the right level removes the source of conflict.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
Developers from one country give very direct feedback in code reviews, and colleagues from another country find it offensive. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate a discussion on cultural differences and agree feedback norms
B) Ask the developers who give direct feedback to stop reviewing their colleagues' code
C) Tell the offended colleagues that direct feedback is normal and should not upset them
D) Separate the two groups so that they no longer review each other's work at all
= A
? Cultural awareness and shared norms resolve the conflict constructively and preserve the benefits of peer review.

@@ PE2 | Agile | multi
Which two observations indicate healthy conflict in a team? (Choose two.)
A) Team members debate ideas openly and focus on the merits of each option
B) Disagreements are raised in meetings rather than discussed behind people's backs
C) Team members make personal remarks about each other's competence
D) Team members stay silent in meetings to avoid any disagreement
E) Decisions are reopened repeatedly through private complaints to the manager
= A, B
? Healthy conflict is open, issue-focused and constructive. Personal attacks, silence and back-channel complaints are signs of unhealthy conflict.

@@ PE3 | Agile
During sprint planning, the project manager believes the developers are committing to far more work than their past velocity suggests. What should the project manager do?
A) Ask questions that help the developers compare the plan with their past velocity
B) Remove stories from the sprint backlog to bring the plan in line with past velocity
C) Say nothing, since the developers own the sprint backlog and may overcommit
D) Tell the product owner to reject the sprint plan until the developers reduce it
= A
? A servant leader coaches through questions so the team makes a better-informed decision, without taking over the developers' ownership of the sprint plan.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
A high-performing engineer asks for more challenging assignments and seems bored by routine tasks. According to McClelland's theory, which need is most evident, and what should the project manager do?
A) Achievement; assign challenging tasks with clear goals and feedback
B) Need for affiliation; assign the engineer to more team-building activities
C) Need for power; give the engineer authority over other team members
D) Need for safety; reassure the engineer about job security
= A
? People with a high need for achievement are motivated by challenging, attainable goals and feedback on performance.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
A reliable team member's work quality has suddenly declined over the last two weeks. What should the project manager do first?
A) Talk privately with the team member to understand what has changed
B) Assign the team member's work to colleagues until the quality improves
C) Inform the functional manager about the decline in performance
D) Remind the whole team about the quality standards at the next meeting
= A
? A sudden change often has a cause the PM does not know about. A private, empathetic conversation comes before any other action.

@@ PE3 | Agile
The team has just delivered a challenging release, but the project has no budget for celebrations. What should the project manager do?
A) Recognise the team's achievement in a low-cost but sincere and personal way
B) Skip any recognition, since there is no budget and the team was only doing its job
C) Wait until the end of the project to recognise all of the team's achievements together
D) Ask the sponsor to send an automated email of thanks to all team members
= A
? Recognition need not be expensive; it should be timely, sincere and meaningful to the team.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
A fire breaks out in a storage area on the project site. Which leadership style is most appropriate in the first minutes?
A) Directive, giving clear instructions based on the emergency procedures
B) Participative, gathering the team's views before deciding what to do
C) Laissez-faire, letting experienced workers decide their own actions
D) Servant leadership, removing impediments for the team to self-organise
= A
? In emergencies, clear and immediate direction based on established procedures protects people. Participative approaches suit situations with time for discussion.

@@ PE3 | Agile
The developers want to use a database technology that is not on the organisation's approved list. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain the constraint and support the team in making a justified exception request
B) Allow the team to use the technology, since self-organising teams choose their own tools
C) Refuse the request without discussion, since the approved list is a mandatory policy
D) Ask the team to use the technology quietly and seek approval later if it works well
= A
? Teams have autonomy within organisational guardrails. The PM explains the constraint and supports a legitimate exception request if the technology is justified.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
The project manager receives an email from a stakeholder harshly criticising the team in front of senior managers. The project manager feels angry. What should the project manager do first?
A) Take time to calm down before responding, then address the concerns factually
B) Reply immediately to all recipients, defending the team in equally strong terms
C) Forward the email to the team so that they understand what is being said about them
D) Ignore the email, since responding would only escalate the situation further
= A
? Self-regulation — managing one's own emotions before acting — is a core part of emotional intelligence and leads to a more effective response.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
A skills assessment shows that only one team member can operate the new testing equipment, which will be needed on several parallel activities. What should the project manager do?
A) Plan cross-training so that more team members can operate the equipment
B) Schedule all testing activities in sequence so the one operator can do them
C) Hire an external operator for the whole duration of the project
D) Ask the operator to work double shifts during the testing period
= A
? Cross-training reduces a single point of failure and increases flexibility. Sequencing everything extends the schedule; double shifts are unsustainable.

@@ PE3 | Agile | multi
A newly formed virtual team has never met in person. Which two actions best help build trust? (Choose two.)
A) Video sessions where members introduce themselves and share their working styles
B) Agreeing working norms, such as response times and core collaboration hours
C) Monitoring each member's keyboard activity to make sure everyone is working
D) Communicating only by email to keep a written record of everything
E) Avoiding personal topics to keep the team strictly professional
= A, B
? Personal connection and clear working agreements build trust in virtual teams. Surveillance and impersonal communication undermine it.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
A pharmaceutical project will be inspected by regulators before the new facility can operate. Which stakeholder group should the project manager engage early in planning?
A) The quality assurance department responsible for regulatory compliance
B) The marketing team that will promote the products made in the facility
C) The finance department that processes the supplier invoices
D) The facilities team that will clean the building after handover
= A
? Quality assurance holds critical knowledge and influence regarding regulatory inspection; engaging it early avoids costly rework.

@@ PE4 | Agile
The team is building a new mobile app for patients, but so far all feedback has come from internal managers. What should the product owner do?
A) Involve real patients through interviews and usability tests on early increments
B) Continue with internal feedback, since managers understand patients' needs well
C) Wait until the app is released and then collect patients' reviews in the app store
D) Ask the developers to imagine how patients would use the app and design for that
= A
? Direct engagement with real users provides the most reliable feedback on value and usability, early enough to act on it.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
The chief financial officer has high power but little interest in the project's details. What is the most appropriate engagement approach?
A) Keep the CFO satisfied with concise updates on financial performance and key decisions
B) Manage the CFO closely by inviting them to every sprint review and design workshop
C) Monitor the CFO with minimal effort, since they show little interest in the project
D) Keep the CFO informed with the full set of detailed project reports every week
= A
? High-power, low-interest stakeholders are kept satisfied with information relevant to their concerns, so they support the project without being overloaded.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
Right after the charter is approved, the project manager wants to identify all stakeholders. Which approach is most effective?
A) Review the charter, agreements and org charts, and consult experts
B) Ask the sponsor to provide a complete list of stakeholders that will not need to change later
C) Wait until stakeholders introduce themselves to the project team during execution
D) Identify only the stakeholders who will approve the project's deliverables at closure
= A
? Stakeholder identification uses documents, expert judgment and data gathering, and continues throughout the project.

@@ PE4 | Agile
The product owner bases backlog decisions only on the opinions of internal colleagues and avoids contact with end users. What should the project manager do?
A) Coach the product owner on the value of direct user feedback and help arrange it
B) Ask the developers to contact end users directly without involving the product owner
C) Replace the product owner with someone who already has contacts with end users
D) Accept the approach, since the product owner is accountable for the backlog decisions
= A
? The PM supports the product owner's effectiveness through coaching and practical help, respecting their accountability.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
A planned pipeline route crosses land traditionally used by an indigenous community. Consultation with the community is legally required. What should the project manager do?
A) Engage the community early and respectfully, following the consultation requirements
B) Begin construction and consult the community once the route has been fixed
C) Ask the legal team to handle the community so that the project team can focus on work
D) Choose a route without informing the community to avoid delays from the consultation
= A
? Legal requirements and ethics both call for early, respectful, genuine engagement with affected communities.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
A stakeholder who was previously a mid-level manager has been promoted to director of the department that will use the system. What should the project manager do?
A) Update the register and adapt the engagement approach to the new role
B) Keep the current engagement approach, since the stakeholder already knows the project well
C) Remove the stakeholder from the register, since directors are represented by the sponsor
D) Ask the sponsor to manage this stakeholder from now on, since they are now senior
= A
? Stakeholder power and interest can change; the register and engagement plan must reflect the current situation.

@@ PE4 | Agile
At sprint reviews, many stakeholders give conflicting feedback at the same time, and the session becomes chaotic. What should happen?
A) The product owner facilitates the review and captures feedback for later prioritisation
B) The developers decide on the spot which feedback to implement in the next sprint
C) The scrum master limits attendance to one stakeholder per review in future
D) The team stops inviting stakeholders and demonstrates the increment to the product owner only
= A
? Sprint reviews benefit from structured facilitation. Feedback is captured and prioritised by the product owner rather than decided on the spot.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
University faculty expect the new student system to reproduce every step of their old paper-based processes. The project plans to adopt standard processes. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain the benefits of standard processes and agree how key needs are met
B) Customise the system so that it reproduces all existing processes exactly as faculty request
C) Implement the standard processes without discussing them with the faculty members first
D) Ask the dean to instruct faculty to accept whatever processes the project delivers
= A
? Aligning expectations involves explaining the rationale and finding how real needs will be met. Silent imposition or full customisation both cause problems.

@@ PE5 | Agile
Stakeholders ask the product owner for an exact delivery date for every feature in a 12-month backlog. What is the best response?
A) Provide a roadmap with forecast ranges and explain how they narrow
B) Provide exact dates for every feature so that stakeholders can plan their own activities
C) Refuse to give any dates, since agile teams do not make forecasts about delivery
D) Ask the developers to estimate every feature in hours so that exact dates can be given
= A
? Adaptive forecasting is honest about uncertainty: near-term items are more certain, and ranges narrow as the team learns.

@@ PE5 | Hybrid
At the end of a release, the customer says the features are not "done" because they are not yet in production. The team considers them done once tested in the staging environment. What should the project manager do?
A) Agree with the customer on an explicit definition of done, including the deployment step
B) Insist that the team's definition is correct, since the team owns the definition of done
C) Deploy the features to production immediately without the planned release activities
D) Ask the customer to accept the features now and discuss deployment at the next release
= A
? Different meanings of "done" signal misaligned expectations. An explicit, shared definition prevents recurring disputes.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
Business users expect that the cut-over to the new billing system will cause no disruption at all. A weekend outage is unavoidable. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain the outage early and agree how it will be managed
B) Avoid mentioning the outage until the week before cut-over to prevent anxiety
C) Promise that there will be no disruption and try to minimise the outage
D) Ask the vendor to tell the business users about the outage on cut-over day
= A
? Early, honest communication about unavoidable impacts allows users to prepare and keeps trust.

@@ PE5 | Agile
Executives expect the move to agile to cut costs by 30% within the first quarter. What should the project manager do?
A) Set realistic expectations focused on value delivery over time
B) Promise the 30% saving so that executives keep supporting the agile transformation
C) Reduce the team size by 30% so that the cost target is met immediately
D) Avoid discussing costs and focus only on the number of features delivered
= A
? Agile delivers value earlier and adapts better, but immediate cost cuts are not guaranteed. Setting realistic expectations protects the initiative's credibility.

@@ PE5 | Hybrid
A customer expects every change request to be decided within one day, while the change process typically takes two weeks. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain the change process and agree a faster route for small, low-impact changes
B) Approve all change requests within one day to satisfy the customer's expectation
C) Tell the customer that the two-week process cannot be changed under any circumstances
D) Stop processing change requests until the customer accepts the standard duration
= A
? Clarifying the process and tailoring it — for instance, delegated approval for minor changes — aligns expectations while keeping control.

@@ PE5 | Predictive | multi
A stakeholder claims that a report was promised as part of the project. Which two documents would best help resolve the disagreement? (Choose two.)
A) The project scope statement
B) The signed agreement or contract
C) The team charter
D) The lessons learned register
E) The resource calendar
= A, B
? The scope statement and the contract define what was agreed to be delivered and excluded.

@@ PE6 | Agile
After release, a stakeholder is unhappy with the colour scheme of the user interface, although it follows the agreed brand guidelines. What should the product owner do?
A) Acknowledge the feedback and consider it in the backlog against other priorities
B) Change the colour scheme immediately to restore the stakeholder's satisfaction
C) Tell the stakeholder that the colours are final and cannot be discussed
D) Ask the designers to justify their choices to the stakeholder in writing
= A
? Feedback is welcomed and evaluated against value and other priorities. Instant changes or flat refusal are both poor expectation management.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
A customer threatens to withhold payment because the project is three weeks late. What should the project manager do?
A) Meet the customer to review causes, recovery plan and contract terms
B) Stop work until the customer confirms that payment will be made on time
C) Ask the legal department to send a formal warning to the customer
D) Promise to recover the three weeks without analysing whether it is feasible
= A
? Open discussion of causes and recovery, grounded in the contract, preserves the relationship and finds a way forward.

@@ PE6 | Hybrid
The engagement assessment shows that a key stakeholder has moved from supportive to neutral over the last month. What should the project manager do?
A) Find out what caused the change and adapt the engagement approach
B) Wait to see whether the stakeholder becomes resistant before acting
C) Ask the sponsor to remind the stakeholder of the project's importance
D) Update the matrix and take no other action, since neutral is acceptable
= A
? Monitoring engagement means acting on changes. Understanding the cause allows a targeted response before support declines further.

@@ PE6 | Agile
A stakeholder asks the team to remove a feature that was built at another stakeholder's request two sprints ago. What should happen?
A) The product owner evaluates it with both stakeholders, based on value
B) The developers remove the feature immediately to satisfy the latest request
C) The scrum master refuses, since completed features cannot be changed
D) The team keeps the feature and ignores the request to avoid conflict
= A
? The product owner balances stakeholder interests and value, and communicates the decision to both stakeholders.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
A stakeholder from the purchasing department asks for the hourly rates of the contractors working on the project. The communications plan restricts this information to the procurement team. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain the restriction and share what is authorised, such as totals
B) Provide the rates, since the stakeholder works in a related department
C) Refuse the request without explaining the reason for the restriction
D) Ask the contractors whether they mind sharing their rates with purchasing
= A
? Following the communications plan protects confidential information while still meeting legitimate information needs where possible.

@@ PE6 | Agile
Velocity dropped by a third during a holiday period, and stakeholders are worried the release will be late. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain the temporary capacity reduction and share an updated forecast
B) Ask the team to work extra hours to restore the previous velocity
C) Report the original forecast unchanged to avoid causing concern
D) Ask the product owner to remove features without telling stakeholders
= A
? Transparent explanation and updated forecasts keep stakeholders' expectations aligned with reality.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
A stakeholder satisfaction survey shows that users find the training materials confusing. What should the project manager do?
A) Analyse the feedback, improve the materials and tell users what was changed
B) Note the feedback in the final report and keep the materials as they are
C) Ask users to read the materials more carefully before the next session
D) Repeat the survey with different questions to obtain better results
= A
? Acting visibly on feedback improves satisfaction and shows stakeholders that their input matters.

@@ PE7 | Hybrid
A research team discovers that an experimental method fails under certain conditions. Other teams in the company may try the same method. What should the project manager do?
A) Capture the finding as a lesson learned and share it with other teams
B) Keep the finding within the team, since failed experiments may reflect badly on it
C) Mention the finding in the final project report at closure
D) Wait for another team to ask before sharing the information
= A
? Sharing knowledge from failures promptly prevents others from repeating them and supports organisational learning.

@@ PE7 | Agile
Several agile teams solve similar testing problems independently, wasting effort. What would best improve knowledge sharing?
A) A community of practice for testing that meets regularly across teams
B) A rule that each team must solve its own problems without outside help
C) A central document listing every team's problems once a year
D) Moving all testers into a single separate testing department
= A
? Communities of practice connect people with similar roles across teams, spreading solutions and good practices.

@@ PE7 | Predictive
A contractor will develop a control system that the client's engineers must maintain for 15 years. How should knowledge transfer be secured?
A) Contract for documentation and training deliverables with acceptance criteria
B) Trust that the contractor will provide documentation if the client asks for it at the end
C) Ask the client's engineers to study the system after the contractor leaves
D) Plan to call the contractor whenever the client has a question in future
= A
? Making knowledge transfer a contracted, verifiable deliverable ensures it happens before final acceptance.

@@ PE7 | Agile
A developer who designed most of the integration layer will leave the team in two weeks. What should the team do?
A) Pair with the developer, record key decisions and hand over open work
B) Ask the developer to finish as many stories as possible before leaving
C) Wait until a replacement is hired and ask the developer to call them
D) Freeze all changes to the integration layer until a new expert joins
= A
? Pairing, decision records and handover of in-progress work transfer both tacit and explicit knowledge before the person leaves.

@@ PE7 | Predictive
In previous lessons learned sessions, people avoided mentioning mistakes, so few useful lessons were recorded. How can the project manager obtain more honest input?
A) Run a blameless, process-focused session, possibly with anonymous input
B) Ask the sponsor to attend the session so that people take it more seriously
C) Require each person to name one mistake they personally made on the project
D) Replace the session with a questionnaire that requires people to sign their answers
= A
? Psychological safety — a blameless, process-focused format — encourages candour and better lessons.

@@ PE7 | Hybrid | multi
Which two methods are most effective for transferring knowledge to 500 users when a new system goes live? (Choose two.)
A) A network of trained super-users in each department
B) Hands-on practice sessions in a training environment
C) Emailing the user manual on the go-live date
D) Providing no training, since the system is intuitive
E) Running the first training session three months after go-live
= A, B
? Super-users provide local, ongoing support, and hands-on practice builds real skills. Manuals alone and late or absent training lead to poor adoption.

@@ PE8 | Predictive
The buyer must formally notify the seller that a contract deliverable has been rejected. Which form of communication is most appropriate?
A) Formal written communication
B) An informal phone call
C) A message in the team chat
D) A comment in the daily stand-up
= A
? Contractual notices require formal, written communication to create a clear record.

@@ PE8 | Agile
Who should normally be invited to the sprint review?
A) The Scrum team and key stakeholders who can give feedback on the increment
B) Only the developers, so that they can review their own work in private
C) Only the product owner and scrum master, to approve the increment formally
D) Only senior executives, so that they can evaluate the team's performance
= A
? The sprint review is a working session in which stakeholders inspect the increment and help adapt the backlog.

@@ PE8 | Hybrid
When should the communications management plan be updated?
A) Whenever stakeholders or their needs change significantly
B) Only at the end of each project phase, at the phase gate
C) Never, once the sponsor has approved it during planning
D) Only when a stakeholder formally complains about communication
= A
? The plan is a living document; it should reflect changes in stakeholders and their needs.

@@ PE8 | Predictive
A project team has 20 members, including the project manager. How many communication channels exist?
A) 190
B) 380
C) 20
D) 210
= A
? Channels = n(n − 1) / 2 = 20 × 19 / 2 = 190.

@@ PE8 | Agile
Remote team members make important technical decisions in private chat messages, and others are often unaware of them. What should the scrum master encourage?
A) Making decisions and discussions visible in shared team channels
B) Banning all private messages between team members
C) Holding a daily meeting to read out every private chat message
D) Asking the project manager to approve every technical decision
= A
? Transparency is an agile pillar; shared channels keep everyone informed without heavy bureaucracy.

@@ PE8 | Predictive
The project will lead to the elimination of several roles in a department. How should the project manager communicate this to the affected employees?
A) In person, together with HR, following the organisation's policies
B) By email, so that every employee receives the same message at once
C) Through the project newsletter, as part of the monthly update
D) Through their team leads informally, before any official decision
= A
? Sensitive, personal news requires face-to-face communication with HR involvement and adherence to policy.

@@ PE8 | Hybrid
The steering committee is confused by the technical detail in the project manager's updates. What should the project manager do?
A) Translate the status into business outcomes, risks and decisions
B) Add a glossary of technical terms to the end of each update
C) Continue as before, since accurate detail is more important than clarity
D) Ask the technical lead to present the updates instead
= A
? Communication should be tailored to the audience. Executives need business-focused information that supports decisions.
